  <title>7.16.4: Sketching A Graph of an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02033</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.4: Sketching A Graph of an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>96c69817-a880-428b-9d82-1659fac64150</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <section>
        <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_7a3f6355-50d8-4553-969c-c2510b2fabfc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para/>
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph defined by \(y=(x−3)^2+2\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 3. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 3.The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a890445c-625a-426a-9e78-080b549637d9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para/>
            <para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph defined by \(y=(x−3)^2+2\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 2. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 2. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Find the coordinates of two other points on the graph. The points should be symmetric across the vertex. Show your reasoning.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\((0,11)\) and \((6,11)\). When \(x\) is 0, the value of \(y\) is \((-3)^2+2\) or 11. The other point is 3 units to the right of the vertex with the same \(y\)-coordinate.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
